1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
In a previous lesson, we established that the SDK and a raw REST call end up in the same place.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
So a fair question is, what does the SDK actually do for you? Well, it handles the unglamorous

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
plumbing and it draws a hard line at capabilities. This lesson walks through both. Let's break it

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:26,000
down. What does that plumbing include? Four jobs stand out. First, authentication. The SDK reads

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
your API key and attaches the right headers to every request. So you set credentials once and

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,000
forget them. Second, retries. When a call hits a transient failure like a rate limit or a temporary

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,000
server error, the SDK retries it automatically with exponential back off. Third, typing. You

8
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,000
get typed arguments and typed response objects. So your editor auto-completes fields and flags

9
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:59,000
mistakes before you run. Fourth, streaming. When you stream a response, the SDK parses the raw

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
event stream into clean message objects. So you're not handwriting a parser. Let's slow down on

11
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
authentication because it's the first thing every integration needs. Every request to Cloud has to

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:17,000
carry a few headers. Your API key goes in an XAPI key header. There's an anthropic version header

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
that pins the API version so your code keeps behaving predictably as the platform evolves.

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
There's a content type header and if you're using a beta feature, an anthropic beta header.

15
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
The SDK sets all of these for you from the client you configure once at startup. You pass your key

16
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
in a single place and from then on every call is authenticated correctly without you thinking

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
about headers again. It's a small thing that removes a whole category of setup bugs.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Next up is the second job, retries, and its close cousin, timeouts. On retries, network calls fail

19
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:00,000
sometimes and not all failures are equal. The SDK knows that certain errors are transient.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
Things like a 429 rate limit or a 500 level server error. For those, it waits and retries

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:13,000
automatically using exponential backoff so it doesn't hammer the service. By default, it retries

22
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:19,000
twice before surfacing the error to you. On timeouts, the SDK applies a default request

23
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
timeout of around 10 minutes and here's a practical detail. For a very long generation,

24
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
it expects you to stream the response rather than block on one enormous reply because a huge

25
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
non-streaming request can run past that limit. Both behaviors are sensible defaults you can tune.

26
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Typing is easy to underrate until it saves you an hour. The SDKs ship full type definitions.

27
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
When you build a request, the parameters are typed so a misspelled field name or a wrong value type

28
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
fails right in your editor before you ever run the code. When the response comes back, it's a

29
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:04,000
typed object, not a raw dictionary you index into with string keys and hope. You read result.content

30
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
and your tools tell you exactly what's there. Autocomplete becomes a live reference for what

31
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
each call accepts and returns. In practice, this means you spend less time flipping to the docs

32
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
and more time writing code and a whole class of silly runtime errors just disappears.

33
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Now here's the boundary I really want to land because it prevents a common mistake.

34
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Everything we've covered is convenience. The SDK doesn't add powers. It exposes only what the

35
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:40,000
underlying REST API supports. If an endpoint can't do something, there is no SDK method that

36
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
can magically make it possible. That matters when capabilities differ by hosting Surface.

37
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:51,000
If a particular server-side tool isn't available on the Surface you're running against, it won't be

38
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
there in that Surface's SDK client either because the client is just calling the same restricted

39
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
endpoint. So whenever you're unsure whether something is possible, don't ask whether the

40
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:09,000
SDK has a method. Ask whether the REST API itself supports it. Let's come back to streaming because

41
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:15,000
it's the clearest example of the SDK saving you real work. When you ask for a streaming response,

42
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:21,000
Claude sends the output as a series of small events over a single connection as the text

43
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,000
generates. If you were working at the raw level, you'd have to read those events yourself, accumulate

44
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:33,000
the text deltas in order, track where each content block starts and stops, and assemble the final

45
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
message. The SDK does all of that for you. You iterate over a clean stream of message objects or

46
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
await the finished result, and the parsing just happens. The important thing to hold on to is that

47
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:51,000
the events on the wire are identical either way. The SDK isn't changing the protocol. It's reading

48
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
it for you. So when would you skip the SDK and call REST directly? The thin wrapper design is

49
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:05,000
exactly what makes this a low-stakes choice. Reach for the SDK by default. In Python or TypeScript,

50
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:12,000
it saves you auth, retries, typing, and streaming parsing with almost no cost. Reach for raw REST

51
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,000
when your language doesn't have an official SDK, when you need the absolute minimum dependency

52
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:23,000
footprint, or when you're debugging and want to see the exact bytes on the wire. And you can mix

53
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:29,000
them freely in one system because both are hitting the same endpoint. Since they're interchangeable,

54
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
you're never locked in. You can start with one and move to the other later at almost no cost.

55
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Let me give you a simple rule to carry out of this lesson. Default to the SDK. If you're building in

56
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Python or TypeScript, the SDK is almost always the right call. It handles authentication, retries,

57
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:55,000
typing, and streaming parsing. And for the vast majority of applications, there's no real downside

58
00:05:55,000 --> 00:06:02,000
to using it. Reach for raw REST in the specific cases we just covered. Your language has no

59
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:07,000
official SDK. You need a minimal dependency footprint, maybe in a constrained environment,

60
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
or you're debugging behavior, and you want to inspect the exact request and response at the

61
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:19,000
wire. Outside those cases, let the SDK carry the plumbing so you can spend your attention on the

62
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:27,000
actual application logic. The SDK gives you a four-part convenience layer over the same API.

63
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:34,000
It handles off, retries, typing, and streaming parsing. Keep in mind, it adds no capability the

64
00:06:34,000 --> 00:06:39,000
REST endpoint lacks. So unsupported in the API means unsupported in the SDK.

65
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
That covers what the SDK handles for you. I'll see you in the next one.

